# Cursor Rules for Mero Enrolment Project

## Project Vision & Core Purpose
You are supporting the development of a web app designed to automate the process of filling PDF forms using data collected via an online form.

### Core Idea:
The app allows users to upload one or more PDF documents (even those with existing form fields), and then design a web-based form interface that mirrors or extends those PDF fields. The user can build a multi-page online form with progress tracking and breadcrumb navigation.

### Key Workflow:
1. User uploads a single or multiple PDF files.
2. The app parses or allows the user to manually mark the fillable fields in the PDFs.
3. The user builds a custom web form (with text inputs, checkboxes, radios, dates, signatures, etc.).
4. The user maps each form field to corresponding fields in the PDF — including support for **repeated fields** (e.g. a name that appears on multiple pages).
5. Users can preview, test-fill, and generate a final filled PDF upon submission.
6. The generated PDF is downloadable or optionally sent somewhere (via API or email).

### Non-Functional Goals:
- Clean, responsive UI/UX that's usable even by non-technical users
- Support for multiple file uploads and mapping to a unified form
- Ability to extend or edit mappings later
- Form logic and field grouping where applicable
- Signature support via drawing or upload

### Purpose of this Project:
Reduce redundant data entry for repeated or complex multi-page PDFs, automate field duplication logic, and streamline document workflows in education, legal, and HR environments.

### Assumptions:
- Users may not know about PDF field naming conventions
- Some PDFs may not have existing form fields; user will manually define them
- We are prioritizing mapping efficiency and clarity

## Project Overview
This is a Next.js 14+ project with TypeScript, using:
- Frontend: Next.js with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui components
- Backend: Node.js/TypeScript server
- PDF handling: react-pdf for client-side PDF viewing and manipulation
- PDF form filling: pdf-lib for programmatic PDF field filling

## General Code Standards

### Next.js 14+ Best Practices
- Always use "use client" directive for components that use React hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.)
- Prefer Server Components by default unless client-side interactivity is needed
- Use App Router patterns (app/ directory structure)
- Follow Next.js 14+ file conventions

### TypeScript Rules
- Always provide proper TypeScript types for props, state, and function parameters
- Use interfaces for component props
- Avoid 'any' type - use proper typing
- Use React.FC for functional components when appropriate

### Component Guidelines
- Use shadcn/ui components when available (Button, Card, Input, etc.)
- Follow consistent naming: PascalCase for components, camelCase for variables
- Keep components focused and single-purpose
- Use proper error handling and loading states

### Styling
- Use Tailwind CSS classes for styling
- Follow responsive design principles
- Use consistent spacing and layout patterns
- Prefer utility classes over custom CSS

### File Structure
- Components go in `frontend/components/ui/` for reusable UI components
- Page components go in `frontend/app/`
- Backend code goes in `backend/src/`

## Specific Rules

### PDF Handling & Form Automation
- Always validate file types before processing
- Use client-side PDF viewing with react-pdf
- Handle PDF loading errors gracefully
- Show appropriate loading states during PDF processing
- Support multiple PDF uploads and field mapping
- Implement PDF field detection and manual field marking
- Enable form field mapping between web forms and PDF fields
- Support repeated fields across multiple pages
- Provide signature capture and drawing capabilities
- Generate filled PDFs using pdf-lib
- Include preview and test-fill functionality
- Support form logic and field grouping
- Prioritize user-friendly interface for non-technical users

### Error Handling
- Always include error boundaries where appropriate
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors appropriately for debugging

### Performance
- Use proper React optimization techniques (memo, useMemo, useCallback)
- Lazy load components when appropriate
- Optimize bundle size by avoiding unnecessary imports

### Security
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize data before rendering
- Follow security best practices for file handling

## Code Review Checklist
Before suggesting code, ensure:
1. ✅ Proper TypeScript types are used
2. ✅ "use client" directive is present if needed
3. ✅ Error handling is implemented
4. ✅ Loading states are considered
5. ✅ Component follows project naming conventions
6. ✅ Tailwind classes are used appropriately
7. ✅ Code is readable and well-commented
8. ✅ No security vulnerabilities are introduced

## Common Patterns to Follow
- Use destructuring for props and state
- Prefer functional components over class components
- Use proper event handling patterns
- Implement proper form validation
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Follow accessibility best practices
- Implement multi-step form navigation with breadcrumbs
- Use drag-and-drop for PDF field mapping
- Provide visual feedback for field mapping relationships
- Include form field grouping and conditional logic
- Support signature capture with drawing canvas
- Implement PDF preview with field highlighting
- Use progress indicators for multi-page workflows
- Provide clear error messages for PDF processing issues

## What to Avoid
- ❌ Using 'any' type in TypeScript
- ❌ Missing "use client" directive for client components
- ❌ Inline styles when Tailwind classes are available
- ❌ Unhandled promises or async operations
- ❌ Hardcoded values that should be configurable
- ❌ Console.log statements in production code
- ❌ Complex PDF field mapping that confuses non-technical users
- ❌ Single-page forms when multi-page with progress tracking is needed
- ❌ Ignoring repeated field scenarios across multiple PDF pages
- ❌ Missing preview functionality before PDF generation
- ❌ Poor error handling for PDF parsing and field detection
